{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman from the Crusader states in the Levant He was born around 1140 and died in 1187 He was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem He succeeded his father as count of Tripoli when he was around 12 years old and ruled under the regency of his mother and his uncle, King Baldwin III of Jerusalem.\n\n## Early Life and Reign\n\nRaymond III was born in 1140 and was the only son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. When Raymond II died in 1152, Raymond III was still a minor, and his mother and uncle, King Baldwin III of Jerusalem, served as regents until he came of age. During his reign, Raymond III faced numerous challenges, including threats from the Zengid Dynasty, a Muslim dynasty that controlled large parts of the Levant. In 1157, Nur ad-Din, the Zengid ruler, besieged the castle of Montferrand, which was held by Raymond III's vassals. Raymond III led a relief army to lift the siege, but he was defeated in battle. This defeat weakened Raymond III's position in the Levant and made him more dependent on the Kingdom of Jerusalem for support.\n\n### Crusade of 1164\n\nIn 1164, Raymond III participated in the Crusade of 1164, led by King Amalric of Jerusalem. The Crusade aimed to capture the city of Banias, which was held by Nur ad-Din. Raymond III played a significant role in the Crusade, leading a contingent of troops from Tripoli. However, the Crusade ultimately failed, and the Crusaders were forced to retreat.\n\n#### Marriage and Children\n\nIn 1157, Raymond III married Eschiva of Bures, the heiress to the Lordship of Banias. The marriage strengthened Raymond III's position in the Levant and gave him control over the strategically important Lordship of Banias. Raymond III and Eschiva had three children together: Bohemond III Count of Tripoli, Raymond IV Count of Tripoli, and Melisende of Tripoli.\n\n##### Death and Legacy\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin, a disastrous defeat for the Crusader states. He was succeeded as count of Tripoli by his eldest son, Bohemond III. Raymond III's reign was marked by numerous challenges, including threats from the Zengid Dynasty and the failure of the Crusade of 1164. However, his marriage to Eschiva of Bures strengthened his position in the Levant and gave him control over the Lordship of Banias. Raymond III is remembered as a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states in the Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -9.107174992095679e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001829695247579366}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.5162993669509888}, {"token": "of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -6.198863957251888e-06}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.15124556422233582}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -0.237417072057724}, {"token": "noble", "logprob": -0.07857891917228699}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.0012342463014647365}, {"token": "from", "logprob": -1.0317106246948242}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "Cr", "logprob": -0.6135440468788147}, {"token": "us", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "states", "logprob": -0.03989730402827263}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -0.012672139331698418}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -0.0010921949287876487}, {"token": "Lev", "logprob": -0.026898840442299843}, {"token": "ant", "logprob": -2.7656173188006505e-05}, {"token": "He", "logprob": -0.023930754512548447}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.000734297966118902}, {"token": "born", "logprob": -0.17792046070098877}, {"token": "around", "logprob": -0.6343296766281128}, {"token": "", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1"